WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:00.849
Most Node.

00:00:00.849 --> 00:00:03.096
js applications rely on npm packages,

00:00:03.096 --> 00:00:06.468
and many of those packages rely on other packages.

00:00:06.468 --> 00:00:09.464
A direct dependency is one your application names.

00:00:09.464 --> 00:00:12.835
A transitive dependency is pulled in by another dependency.

00:00:12.835 --> 00:00:18.453
The full dependency tree can become much larger than the code a team intentionally wrote,

00:00:18.453 --> 00:00:23.323
which means supply chain security has to be part of normal application security.

00:00:23.323 --> 00:00:27.443
Lockfiles help make dependency versions more reproducible across installs and environments.

00:00:27.443 --> 00:00:30.065
They are not a guarantee of safety,

00:00:30.065 --> 00:00:33.810
but they reduce ambiguity about what code should be installed.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:38.305
When lockfiles are ignored, regenerated casually, or allowed to drift without review,

00:00:38.305 --> 00:00:40.927
teams can lose visibility into what changed.

00:00:40.927 --> 00:00:46.171
Dependency updates should be intentional enough to support both security fixes and operational stability.

00:00:46.171 --> 00:00:49.542
Supply chain risks include vulnerable packages, abandoned packages, typosquatting,

00:00:49.542 --> 00:00:53.662
dependency confusion, and package scripts that execute during installation or build.

00:00:53.662 --> 00:00:57.782
These topics do not require fear, but they do require governance.

00:00:57.782 --> 00:01:02.652
Teams should know which package registries are trusted, how new dependencies are approved,

00:01:02.652 --> 00:01:07.146
what scripts are allowed in CI/CD, and how vulnerability alerts are triaged.

00:01:07.146 --> 00:01:10.517
The answer is not to write everything from scratch.

00:01:10.517 --> 00:01:13.514
That usually creates more risk and maintenance burden.

00:01:13.514 --> 00:01:17.634
The practical answer is to minimize unnecessary dependencies, prefer well-maintained packages,

00:01:17.634 --> 00:01:21.754
use software composition analysis, review dependency changes, pin and update responsibly,

00:01:21.754 --> 00:01:26.249
and design the application so a compromised or flawed package has less

00:01:26.249 --> 00:01:29.620
privilege and less access than it would by default.
